            "text": "At dawn, the city belongs to people who are usually invisible. Bakers lift metal shutters and release warm air into empty streets. Bus drivers sip coffee at red lights. A nurse walks home beneath windows where strangers are beginning to dream less deeply. Even the pigeons seem quieter, as if they understand that morning is a fragile agreement.\n\nI like this hour because nothing has fully decided what it will become. The caf\u00e9 tables are clean, the headlines unread, the park paths striped with long shadows. Possibility does not arrive with trumpets; it waits beside a locked shop, wearing yesterday\u2019s rain on its sleeves.\n\nSoon, noise gathers. Trains pull thousands of private worries toward offices. Phones brighten. Deliveries thump onto doorsteps. Someone misses an appointment, someone accepts a proposal, and someone decides, without telling anyone, to begin again. By noon, the city appears certain of itself, though certainty is mostly speed viewed from a distance.\n\nStill, dawn leaves a residue. It reminds me that enormous systems depend on small gestures: a key turned, a kettle filled, a uniform buttoned in the blue half-light. History often celebrates speeches, victories, and inventions, but ordinary continuance is its hidden engine.\n\nTomorrow, before the traffic thickens, the same quiet workers will return. Bread will rise, headlights will cross wet pavement, and the first bus will kneel at the curb. The day will open without ceremony, offering everyone another unfinished sentence. We may fill it badly or well, but for one clear moment, it waits."
